# BatchFixes-1 (2026-10-30): the two latent findings of the 2026-10-30 upstream batch, on the hunt stack (applies at
# the END of stack/all6r.list; the same lines as the batch's 0134 / 0135 and the new sync ports).
# 1. indexer_shard.scratch_for: `got.rows < sel.rows` could never hold (the ShardScratch is made with sel.rows and
#    cached on that very SelectScratch, whose rows never change; scratch_for is the only writer of sel.shard): the
#    clause goes. What it stood for, a window of more rows than the scratch, was unguarded in the sharded path
#    (seg_select_tokens refuses it, seg_shard_rank did not): driven on CPU with a 5-row window on a 4-row scratch at
#    1,048,576 tokens of context, the kernels wrote past the scratch (heap corruption, SIGSEGV). seg_shard_rank now
#    refuses it with seg_select_tokens' message. The serve's windows are sliced from buffers of the scratch's rows, so
#    production never hit it.
# 2. tp.expert_owners (TF_GLM_EXPERT_ROTATE): a rotation over a checkpoint whose mlp_layer_types is not one entry a
#    layer (num_hidden_layers) is refused, naming the field and both lengths, by both layouts (from_text, from_config)
#    and so before the loader opens a weight file; before, a short list left the last layers unrotated. Off: unchanged.
# Checks: upstream/glm-pr-indexer-shard and glm-pr-expert-rotate (their check tools and mutants.sh).
